## Why This Is Asked
Understanding cross-language adapter strategies with strict latency and memory constraints.

## Key Concepts
- PEFT vs full fine-tuning in multi-language QA
- Adapter injection points (attention vs MLP)
- Language tagging vs per-language adapters
- Latency/memory budgeting and rollback plans

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo adapter injection sketch
function attachAdapters(model, keys) {
  for (const m of model.modules()) {
    if (m instanceof AttentionLayer || m instanceof MLP) {
      m.addAdapter({rank: 8, name: keys.next()});
    }
  }
  return model;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you monitor drift between languages post-deployment?
- What failure modes require bypassing adapters and using base model instead?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical understanding of tokenization-to-attention flow in a real task like headline classification. It connects a toy numeric example to concepts used in production.

## Key Concepts
- Self-attention with Q, K, V
- Scaling by sqrt(dk) and softmax
- How token representations influence weights and output

## Code Example
```javascript
function attention(q, K, V) {
  // q: 1xd, K: nxd, V: nxd
  const dk = q.length;
  const scores = K.map(k => dot(q, k) / Math.sqrt(dk));
  const weights = softmax(scores);
  const out = [0,0].map((_,i) => weights.reduce((acc, w, idx) => acc + w * V[idx][i], 0));
  return {weights, out};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to multi-head attention and what changes in compute cost?
- How do embedding size and padding affect numerical stability and softmax behavior?

Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates hands-on design of a real-time RAG system, including multi-embedding chunking, multi-stage retrieval, and data freshness in production-like conditions.

Key Concepts
- Dual-embedding chunking
- Vector DB indexing and cosine search
- Cross-encoder reranking
- Streaming updates and TTL pruning
- Latency targets and observability

Code Example
```javascript
async function retrieve(query) {
  const eSent = await embed(query, 'sentence');
  const eSect = await embed(query, 'section');
  const topSent = vectorDB.search(eSent,  topK);
  const topSect = vectorDB.search(eSect, topK);
  // fuse and rerank
  return rerank(topSent, topSect, query);
}
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you measure and improve retrieval quality in production?
- How would your design adapt if the vector DB spans multiple regions with different latencies?

## Why This Is Asked
Enterprise chat assistants must process multilingual, long-context inputs containing specialized domain terminology while maintaining performance. This question evaluates understanding of tokenizer design, attention mechanisms, and practical engineering trade-offs in production systems.

## Key Concepts
- Multilingual subword tokenization with domain-specific vocabulary enhancement
- Sparse attention patterns using local windows and global anchors
- Cross-language semantic alignment and retrieval augmentation
- Latency-accuracy trade-offs in large-scale deployment

## Code Example
```javascript
function buildSparseMask(n, localWin, globalAnchors) {
  const mask = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(false));

  // Local attention windows
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    const start = Math.max(0, i - localWin);
    const end = Math.min(n, i + localWin + 1);
    for (let j = start; j < end; j++) {
      mask[i][j] = true;
    }
  }

  // Global anchor attention
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (const anchor of globalAnchors) {
      mask[i][anchor] = true;
      mask[anchor][i] = true;
    }
  }

  return mask;
}
```
